WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.581 --> 00:00:05.580
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Welcome,
and thanks for coming.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.550
Today we're going to talk about
running Node.js applications

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.260
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.210
We're going to take a look at
Google Cloud, build a few apps,

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:16.370
and learn about using Google's
advanced APIs and services

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.160
with your applications.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.760
But before we dive in, a
little bit about myself.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.340
My name is Justin Beckwith,
and I'm a product manager here

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.210
at Google.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.270
My contact information
is available here,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.270
so feel free to
reach out at any time

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.980
if you have questions or
feedback about running Node.js

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.640
applications on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.360
As someone who spent
years as a web developer,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.040
I've found that there's a
lot to like about Node.js.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.430
Many developers spend
years writing JavaScript

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.270
for fun and web applications.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.210
One of the best parts of Node.js
is that it's just JavaScript.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.640
The same skills you use
to develop applications

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.790
in the browser can be used to
develop amazing applications

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.010
in the cloud as well.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.650
It's easy to get ramped
up, as the core of Node.js

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.960
is very small.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.670
Many of the libraries are
going to use our open source

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.920
and have been
developed by community.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.160
Last month, there were
over 4 billion packages

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.120
downloaded through NPM, the
Node.js package manager.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.760
This is a sign of a
community that's growing.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.280
As the Node.js community
continues to grow,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.850
it's becoming a
great destination

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.370
for enterprise focused
web applications.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:24.290
Node.js offers a mature API and
an LTS release schedule that

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.790
provides 18 months of support.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.130
Over that same time,
Google Cloud Platform

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.720
has also grown and become
a great destination

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.460
for web applications and
APIs that need Google scale.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.780
Node.js is built on V8,
the same JavaScript engine

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.330
that powers Google Chrome.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.630
We have years of experience
tuning JavaScript engines

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.670
to provide incredible
performance in the browser.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.150
And now we're helping
deliver that same incredible

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.260
performance in the cloud.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.684
Google Cloud Platform
is also easy to use.

00:01:53.684 --> 00:01:55.100
You can bring your
code and let us

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.570
handle the scale and the ops.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.830
Finally, we offer a variety
of APIs and services

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.780
that allow you to take advantage
of Google's incredible big data

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.860
and machine learning technology.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.850
The same infrastructure
in APIs that

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.470
make it possible to run services
like Google Search, Gmail,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.520
and Google Photos are all
now at your fingertips,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.640
helping you bring your
applications to life.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.830
There are three easy ways
to run your applications

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.220
on Google Cloud.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.600
App Engine is Google's
powerful, easy to use

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.110
platform as a service.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.800
You bring your code and
we'll handle the rest.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.110
App Engine will automatically
scale your application

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.510
to handle spikes in traffic,
provide no-downtime upgrades,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.030
and make it easy
to take advantage

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.380
of our advanced diagnostics
tools and production.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.250
Container Engine
is a hosted version

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.480
of Kubernetes, which
brings Google's container

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.000
orchestration
technology to the cloud.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.960
It's incredibly powerful for
applications that are already

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.020
built on top of Docker.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.090
And finally, if you
need complete control

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.180
and flexibility,
Compute Engine offers

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.910
traditional virtual
machines that you

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.100
can manage and control.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.170
Now that we know a little bit
about Google Cloud and Node.js,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.140
let's build a simple
web application

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.910
and deploy it to App Engine.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:07.690
You can get started using any
Node.js framework you'd like,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.860
but today we're
going to use Express,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.730
a very common module used
for building web applications

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.590
and APIs.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.520
I'm going to start by
scaffolding a new web

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.580
application using Express.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.110
We're going to install our
dependencies locally and take

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.720
a quick look at
the app in action.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.390
This is a simple application.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.860
Now let's get it up and
running on App Engine.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.290
To get started, you need
to create a new project

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.690
in the Google Cloud
developer console.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.020
I'm going to deploy to a
project I created earlier

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.130
using the Google Cloud SDK.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.040
The gcloud preview
app deploy command

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.170
will publish your code
to Google App Engine,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.290
install any NPM dependencies
in your package.json,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.010
and start your app.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.880
Under the hood, we're
creating cloud load balancers,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.620
virtual machines, auto scalers,
managed instance groups,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.160
virtual networks,
everything you need

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.700
to build a great performing
application with a great SLA.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.780
Now that our application
has been deployed,

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.060
let's check it out.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.940
I'm going to run gcloud preview
app browse to take a look

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.540
at my site in production.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.360
And here you have it, a
simple Node.js application

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.380
with Express running
on Google App Engine.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.019
Now up to this point, we've
been using the Cloud SDK

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.209
to work on our app.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.820
Let's take a look at how we can
use the Google Cloud developer

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.920
console to help manage it.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.900
Here we can see the
App Engine dashboard,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.780
which provides a heads-up view
of our application performance

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.680
metrics.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.520
There's a lot to
explore with App Engine,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.020
but some of the
most useful features

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.750
I want to talk about today
are under the Versions

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.230
tab in the developer console.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.630
App Engine makes it easy
to simultaneously run

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.940
multiple versions
of your application.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.420
This is useful in
a few situations.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.150
First, it's great for performing
no-downtime deployments,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.210
where you want to test a
new version of your app

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.174
before sending any users
to the new deployment.

00:04:54.174 --> 00:04:56.590
You can see that I have a few
versions of this app already

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:57.420
deployed.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.380
Here's the new version
we just deployed,

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.650
and the previous
version we were running.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.390
This is a great feature if
you need high availability

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:06.810
and no-downtime upgrades.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.770
In our case, we were
routing 100% of our traffic

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:10.771
to a new version.

00:05:10.771 --> 00:05:12.520
But what if you want
to gradually roll out

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:13.230
your changes?

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.760
Or what if you want to
perform AB testing and route

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.160
different users to distinct
versions of your application?

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.450
Traffic splitting makes enabling
these kinds of setups simple.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.450
As we saw, getting started
with Node.js on App Engine

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:27.950
is really easy.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.870
You can bring your
code and let us run it

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.490
on Google's infrastructure.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.380
You can automatically
scale to millions

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.490
of requests per second, and
you don't need to have downtime

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.260
during your deployments.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.410
Now you may be wondering,
what if I decide

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.300
to use Container Engine, or
I want to move my application

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.260
on-premise?

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.120
Well, I've got great news.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.080
The same Docker image we used
to run your application on App

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.090
Engine can be used
with Container Engine,

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.560
or Kubernetes, or any
other service that

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.410
can run Docker containers.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.750
Our last application
was really simple.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.600
It was just a single
web application

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.010
with no real backend.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.060
In reality, our apps
are rarely this simple.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.210
They're often more complex,
because the problems

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.580
we're trying to solve for our
customers are also complex.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:13.370
We need databases like
MongoDB, Postgres, or Datastore

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:14.650
to store our content.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.370
We need message queues
for connecting services.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:20.950
We need advanced services, like
the Cloud Vision API and Cloud

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.960
ML to help us understand
the world around us.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.340
We use advanced tools like
BigQuery and Data Flow

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.400
to process millions of records
to gain valuable insights

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.590
on our products.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.770
On Google Cloud Platform,
you can combine all of these.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.860
Google products,
open source products,

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.320
managed services,
bring your own,

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.320
use what you want to
build the experiences

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.270
that your customers need.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.550
When you're ready to take
advantage of Google's APIs

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:48.380
and services, you can use
the gcloud NPM module.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.580
This NPM module gives you
access to over a dozen Google

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.530
APIs and services,
with more on the way.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.100
It works from within App Engine,
Kubernetes, Compute Engine,

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.620
even from applications that
aren't deployed to Google Cloud

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:01.990
Platform.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.960
In my experience,
these are the services

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.550
that make Google magic.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:08.180
Containers, the
Cloud Vision API,

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:09.750
BigQuery, Machine Learning.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.690
We can use these
amazing technologies

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.350
to build applications that
serve billions of people

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:15.830
all over the planet.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:19.380
Let's take a look at how you
can use these to get started.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.040
Next, we're going to take a
look at a fun little Node.js

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:25.470
application that uses
the gcloud NPM module.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.400
This app uses App Engine, Cloud
Storage, the Cloud Vision API,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.700
and Cloud Pub/Sub to analyze
images from the internet,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.310
separate them into pictures
of dogs on the right,

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.320
and cats on the left.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:38.940
So now let's take
a look at how we

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.940
can use these APIs with
the gcloud NPM module

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.330
to build this kind
of application.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.500
To store our images, we're
using Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:50.000
Cloud storage provides
a scalable file store,

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.830
great for images, videos, or
other large files that you want

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:55.600
to keep out of your database.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.800
Using the Cloud
Storage API is simple.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.420
You can even use it to
serve static HTTP content.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.920
Next up is the Cloud Vision API.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.160
Using this simple service,
with five lines of code,

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.940
we can detect common
labels, landmarks,

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.520
and even facial
expressions within an image

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.510
almost instantaneously.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.460
Finally, we're
using Cloud Pub/Sub

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.710
to send messages between
the microservices that

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.280
make up this application.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.160
Cloud Pub/Sub provides low
latency durable messaging

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.320
between services or devices.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.370
Using these APIs, we can
take advantage of technology

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.730
that's been in development
at Google for years.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:33.270
Whether it's our machine
learning technology, databases,

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.270
or big data tools,
we have services

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:39.260
that make building amazing
applications fast and scalable.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.299
Of course, sometimes you're
going to run into trouble.

00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:43.880
We've all been in
a situation where

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:45.760
code works great
on our machines,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:47.880
but for one reason or
another, starts giving us

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.380
trouble in production.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.740
Traditionally, debugging
these kinds of problems

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.550
has been very difficult.
But with the Stackdriver

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.770
diagnostics tools,
we can dig deep

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.670
into performance and quality
issues in production.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.570
Let's take a look at
Stackdriver Trace in action.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.140
In the developer
console, I'm going

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.030
to navigate to the Trace UI.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.310
This will show an overview
of the overall latency

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.950
for requests to
my HTTP endpoints.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.200
I'm going to drill into
the Traces view, which

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.130
provides a detailed breakdown
of every single outgoing HTTP

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.920
and RPC call coming from my app.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.390
As you can see, this last
request took around 10 seconds.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:24.890
That's a long time.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.270
Let's find out why.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.490
I can see that we're
downloading a lot of images

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.770
to run through the
Cloud Vision API.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.470
You can see every
request we're making

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.460
to download images, upload
them to cloud storage,

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:39.300
and analyze them with
the Cloud Vision API.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.020
You can even click on a request
to get a drill-down view that

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.740
shows the origin of the request
in your application source

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.020
code.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.500
This is a great way to
diagnose performance issues

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:49.870
in production.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.890
But what if instead
of a performance issue

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:53.600
you're facing a crash?

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.020
Stackdriver Debugger
makes it easy to analyze

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:58.770
the state of your
application in production.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.010
The debug console allows
you to set breakpoints

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:02.120
in your production code.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:04.710
Now, this isn't a
traditional breakpoint.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.710
When a request hits
this code path,

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:08.620
it will analyze the
state of your application

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:12.180
passively, allowing you to
capture state without affecting

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.630
performance in production.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.280
In review, Stackdriver
diagnostic tools

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.290
make it easy to diagnose
issues in production.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.240
We looked at Trace
and Debug, but there

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.280
are a variety of other tools
at your disposal as well.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.850
Today we covered a lot.

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.600
We looked at how
App Engine is making

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.860
it easy to operate
at Google Scale.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.370
We looked at using
Google's APIs and services

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.640
to build amazing applications.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.820
We diagnosed problems
in production,

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:42.220
and of course, we talked about
how Google Cloud and Node.js

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.050
are a great match.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:46.740
Everything we looked at
today is open source.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.780
The Docker images we
used to build the Node.js

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:52.980
runtime, the gcloud NPM module,
the Trace and Debug tools,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:56.980
these are all open source
and available on GitHub.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:58.880
I'd like to thank
you again for coming.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.950
And as I mentioned, please
feel free to reach out

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.400
if you have any questions.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.910
You can learn more about
building Node.js applications

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:07.685
on Google Cloud Platform
at cloud.google.com/nodejs.

